Type Bits Decimal precision Use in CP <code>float</code> 32 ~6–7 digits Almost never — insufficient <code>double</code> 64 ~15–17 digits Standard for geometry, probability, real answers <code>long double</code> 80–128 (platform) ~18–21 digits High-precision geometry, cumulative sums <p>Pitfall. <code>double</code> cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly — <code>0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3</code>. Never compare doubles with <code>==</code>. Use an epsilon:</p> <pre><code>const double EPS = 1e-9;
if (abs(a - b) &lt; EPS) { /* a ≈ b */ }
</code></pre> <p>For values spanning many orders of magnitude, use a relative tolerance:</p> <pre><code>if (abs(a - b) &lt; EPS * max(1.0, max(abs(a), abs(b)))) { /* a ≈ b */ }
</code></pre> <p>Pitfall. In geometry, summing $10^6$ doubles each ~$10^{-3}$ can lose ~3 decimal digits of precision to accumulated rounding. If the problem demands high precision, use <code>long double</code>. If it's borderline, Kahan summation can help, but the correct fix is usually to restructure the computation.</p>
